## Performance Review Answers

### What are your top strengths?

My top strengths are empathy, articulation, and speed. People remember me for how I listen to what teams actually need—not what they think they need—and translate that into clear, actionable solutions. When FARR was facing a billion-dollar compliance risk, I didn't just hear "we need extraction"; I understood the urgency, the constraints, and the long-term vision, then delivered both a short-term solution that exceeded expectations and a strategic roadmap. I'm known for explaining complex technical concepts in ways that executives, engineers, and business stakeholders all understand, which is why I can resolve architectural conflicts and influence decisions across teams. My speed comes from knowing frameworks and seeing all the pieces of a system we need, but I'm not held back by red tape or overengineering—I build what works, ship it, and iterate. When ground truth was blocking 5+ teams and had been kicked down the road multiple times, I didn't wait for perfect processes; I created usable ground truth, built correction systems, and established it as an enterprise pillar. I think strategically but execute pragmatically, which is how I unified fragmented AI efforts across multiple teams into a coherent strategy while actually delivering production results.

### What impact(s) did you have on the business?

I've delivered impact where it matters most: protecting the company from risk, saving millions in costs, and accelerating how fast teams can ship AI. When FARR faced a billion-dollar compliance exposure with new FinCEN requirements, I didn't just meet the deadline—I delivered a solution that exceeded their accuracy expectations by 20% and came in three months early, all while building the long-term architecture. The data extraction service I launched addresses $2.5MM a year in manual processing costs for one use case, and by increasing automation coverage from 60% to 100% and building a self-service platform, I've created a template that can enable 15+ similar initiatives across the company—potentially saving $30MM+ annually. What I'm most proud of is how I've accelerated the entire organization. I created the Ground Truth pillar and own extraction end-to-end—both the short-term solutions teams need now and the long-term strategy that shapes where we're going. I unified fragmented efforts across DMS, Platform AI, DES, SRP, and FARR into one coherent strategy. And I raised capability through training that grew from 3 to 50+ attendees, preventing anti-patterns and enabling others to replicate success. The AI Gateway's 100% enterprise adoption eliminated illicit LLM use risk and standardized how we safely deploy AI. These aren't isolated wins—they compound: better ground truth enables better extraction, which enables more automation, which drives more value. I build systems that make everyone faster.

### What areas do you still want to develop, and what steps can you take to do so?

I want to expand my business domain fluency so I can better spot high-impact automation opportunities and strategically guide where we invest in enterprise AI. Right now I'm strong on the technical side, but I need deeper understanding of the business problems teams are actually solving. I'll partner with SRP domain SMEs and shadow operational teams to see the work firsthand—not just hear about it in meetings. I also want to formalize and scale the system mapping process I started with the open order workflow mapping. That session clarified ambiguous processes for multiple groups and revealed automation opportunities nobody had seen before. I want to make that a repeatable process we use across the organization. On the team building side, I'm focused on building systems for all our PMs that integrate with design and engineering processes and leverage automation tools like BMAD to uplevel our entire organization. My goal is to use my position as an AI leader to become a stronger product leader—developing greater organizational influence and executive presence. I'll do this by continuing to translate technical complexity into strategic guidance, resolving cross-team conflicts, and creating frameworks that enable others to succeed at scale.

### Rate your ability to demonstrate your competencies/job requirements for your specific role.

I've consistently demonstrated competencies that exceed my current role level, operating at a Director/Staff level across multiple dimensions. As a Product Manager, I'm expected to manage roadmaps, prioritize features, and work cross-functionally—but I've gone far beyond that. I manage two separate AI roadmaps (Documents AI and EagleAI platform), shape enterprise-wide strategy, and translate technical complexity into strategic guidance for the CTO and Head of Engineering. I've created foundational infrastructure—the Ground Truth pillar, extraction strategy, evaluation loop—that serves as the foundation for FA's entire AI platform, work that typically falls to senior architects or platform leads. I've unified fragmented efforts across 6+ teams into a coherent enterprise strategy, resolved architectural conflicts, and influenced vendor and build-vs-buy decisions—responsibilities that typically require Director-level authority. I've delivered production results that protect the company from billion-dollar risks and enable millions in cost savings, while simultaneously building long-term strategic architecture. My ability to lead through influence, build systems that scale across teams, and execute pragmatically while thinking strategically demonstrates competencies well beyond a standard PM role. I've proven I can operate at a Director/Staff level by consistently taking ownership of ambiguous, high-stakes problems, building enterprise infrastructure, and accelerating the entire organization's AI capability.

### Did you live out the FA Behavior: "Step Up for Customers"?

Yes. When FARR came to me with new FinCEN form requirements that would expose the company to up to a billion dollars in liability if we didn't meet the deadline, I didn't hesitate. They needed help, and the timeline was tight. Rather than choosing between a quick fix or long-term solution, I did both—I started vendor interviews for the strategic vision while building a short-term extraction solution that exceeded their accuracy expectations by 20% and delivered three months ahead of schedule. This wasn't just about meeting a deadline; it was protecting the company from massive regulatory risk while building toward something sustainable. I treated their emergency as my priority and delivered results that exceeded what they asked for.

### Did you live out the FA Behavior: "Act Like an Owner"?

Yes. I consistently take ownership of problems that aren't technically mine but are blocking progress. When I discovered that 5+ teams were stalled on AI apps because they lacked good ground truth—something multiple teams had tried to solve and kicked down the road—I didn't wait for someone else to fix it. I created the first set of 100 documents of ground truth that actually worked for evaluation, built a system where teams could correct their own ground truth, documented the process, and then took it further by establishing Ground Truth as a foundational pillar of our enterprise AI platform. Now teams can proactively collect, correct, generate, and access labeled ground truth on demand. Similarly, when enterprise extraction needed someone to own it, I stepped up—building the first high-accuracy extractor in production, supporting 6+ teams including SRP (top priority), and shaping the long-term roadmap. I see gaps that block progress and fill them, even when they're not in my job description.

### Did you live out the FA Behavior: "Pay It Forward"?

Yes. I've made raising organizational AI capability a core part of my work. I led a 3-part AI training series (Getting Started, Prompt Engineering, CustomGPT) that started with 3 attendees and grew to regularly over 50. But it's not just about teaching—I prevented anti-patterns and redundant questions by showing concrete examples of how to build successfully, so others could replicate that success. I've created reusable frameworks, documentation, prompt libraries, and onboarding guides that teams actually use to build their own AI solutions. When I saw that every engineer was trying to build their own AI apps with no standard framework—leading to bad, wasteful, and potentially harmful implementations—I didn't just build better systems myself; I created the AI Gateway and standardized processes so everyone could deploy AI safely and effectively. My goal is to make the organization better, not just deliver my own projects.

### Did you live out the FA Behavior: "Solve Problems"?

Yes. I consistently tackle ambiguous, high-stakes problems that others avoid or defer. When enterprise extraction had no clear owner and teams were struggling, I took responsibility—building the first high-accuracy extractor in production, supporting 6+ teams including SRP (the company's top priority), and shaping the long-term extraction roadmap. When ground truth was blocking 5+ teams and had been kicked down the road multiple times, I solved it systematically—creating usable ground truth, building correction systems, and establishing it as an enterprise platform pillar. When I saw fragmentation across DMS, Platform AI, DES, SRP, and FARR with divergent approaches creating duplication, I didn't just identify the problem—I consolidated them into a unified architecture and roadmap. I also did the first end-to-end mapping of ambiguous workflows like the open order back-office process, which clarified business processes for multiple downstream groups and revealed automation opportunities nobody had seen before. I solve problems that create forward motion for the entire organization.

### Did you live out the FA Behavior: "Lead the Way"?

Yes. I lead through influence, strategy, and technical excellence rather than formal authority. I manage two separate AI roadmaps, oversee document extraction across both services, and shape both short and long-term strategy. I translate technical complexity into strategic guidance for leadership—including the CTO and Head of Engineering—helping them make informed decisions about vendor choices, build-vs-buy strategies, and architectural directions. I resolve architectural conflicts across teams, influence vendor decisions, and create onboarding and rollout strategies. When I built Docs2Data as FA's first production AI agent, I wasn't just delivering a solution—I was establishing the reference architecture that defines how all AI agents should look in production. I set direction through example, frameworks, and strategic thinking that others follow.

### Did you live out the FA Behavior: "Take Intelligent Risks"?

Yes. I take calculated risks that enable significant future value. When I did the first end-to-end mapping of the open order back-office workflow, I was venturing into ambiguous territory—clarifying business processes that multiple groups found confusing and identifying automation opportunities that weren't immediately obvious. This mapping work revealed future automation opportunities and provided clarity for multiple downstream groups. Similarly, when I saw that an agency outside the company had organized our information well with their AgentNet knowledge resource and ANA chatbot, I took the risk of bringing them into the fold rather than building from scratch—recognizing that collaboration and integration would serve the company better than starting over. This became the first AI app ever launched at FA. I take risks that are informed by deep understanding and strategic thinking, not reckless gambles.

### Did you live out the FA Behavior: "Row Together"?

Yes. Collaboration and unification are central to how I work. I unified fragmented AI efforts across DMS, Platform AI, DES, SRP, Sequoia, and other teams into a single coherent enterprise strategy—eliminating duplication and giving the company clear direction. I helped establish the new pillars-based approach to AI that focuses on solving key user problems, bringing teams together around shared infrastructure rather than competing solutions. When I worked with an agency technically outside the company to bring AgentNet and ANA into core First American, I recognized that their organized information was better than building from scratch—so I brought them into the fold and started collapsing team structures to work together more effectively. I've supported 6+ teams on extraction, resolved cross-team dependencies, and created systems like the AI Gateway that require everyone to work together using shared standards. My goal is always to make the whole organization stronger, not just deliver my individual contributions.

